  "prompt_spec": {
    "original_prompt": "From Aristotle's works, it is clear that phantasia is causally downstream from aisthesis ontologically: phantasia ontologically depends on aisthesis but is different from it. Articulate the ontological chain of causal dependency, as aristotle understands it, of the following (each \"-->\" illustrates the following item being causally downstream): motion-->time-->aisthesis-->phantasia-->resonant aisthema and pathos -->phantasmata-->thinking, recollection, imagination, etc.",
    "research_questions": [
      "What is the ontological chain of causal dependency in Aristotle's philosophy from motion through time, aisthesis, phantasia, resonant aisthema and pathos, phantasmata, to higher cognitive functions like thinking, recollection, and imagination?"
    ],
    "required_facets": [
      {
        "facet_id": "6ba8186b-eb39-4ad1-bf2c-0fefaa7f5e8e",
        "name": "Motion and Time as Foundational Ontological Principles",
        "description": "How does Aristotle establish motion (kinesis) as the ontological foundation for time (chronos) in Physics Book IV and other works? What is the causal relationship whereby time depends on motion as the measure or number of motion with respect to before and after? How does this foundational metaphysical relationship establish the basis for all subsequent psychological and perceptual processes in Aristotle's natural philosophy, particularly as discussed in Physics, Metaphysics, and De Anima?",
        "facet_role": "core",
        "evidence_policy_for_kind": {},
        "archived": false,
        "atoms_mode": "analytics",
        "reverse_check_mode": "warn"
      },
      {
        "facet_id": "b56e439d-40e0-4f28-a8b0-51541a5d7102",
        "name": "Aisthesis Dependence on Motion and Time",
        "description": "How does Aristotle argue in De Anima and De Sensu that sense perception (aisthesis) causally depends on motion and time? What role does the actuality of sensible forms, the movement of sensory media, and temporal duration play in making perception possible? How does Aristotle explain that perception requires kinetic processes in the sense organs and perceptual medium, and why must perception occur in time rather than instantaneously, as discussed in De Anima Books II-III and De Sensu et Sensibilibus?",
        "facet_role": "core",
        "evidence_policy_for_kind": {},
        "archived": false,
        "atoms_mode": "analytics",
        "reverse_check_mode": "warn"
      },
      {
        "facet_id": "1e7c0aed-be94-4d6c-8259-b7b23e354011",
        "name": "Phantasia's Ontological Dependence on Aisthesis",
        "description": "How does Aristotle establish in De Anima Book III chapters 3 and 11, De Memoria, and De Insomniis that phantasia (imagination or appearance) is causally downstream from and ontologically dependent on aisthesis (sense perception), while remaining distinct from it? What arguments does Aristotle provide that phantasia cannot occur without prior perception, that it involves a movement resulting from actual perception, and how does he distinguish phantasia from both perception and thought? What is the nature of the causal mechanism whereby aisthesis produces phantasia?",
        "facet_role": "core",
        "evidence_policy_for_kind": {},
        "archived": false,
        "atoms_mode": "analytics",
        "reverse_check_mode": "warn"
      },
      {
        "facet_id": "f567c632-b0d4-4ea4-8fbf-761f6d2266e8",
        "name": "Resonant Aisthema and Pathos as Intermediary Affections",
        "description": "What is the role of resonant aisthema (sense-impression or affection) and pathos (affective state or passion) in Aristotle's causal chain between phantasia and phantasmata? How do these affective and perceptual residues function as intermediary stages in De Anima, De Memoria, Rhetoric, and Nicomachean Ethics? How does Aristotle explain the persistence of perceptual affections after the original perception ceases, and what is their relationship to both phantasia and the production of phantasmata (mental images)? What role do pleasure and pain play in this causal process?",
        "facet_role": "supporting",
        "evidence_policy_for_kind": {},
        "archived": false,
        "atoms_mode": "analytics",
        "reverse_check_mode": "warn"
      },
      {
        "facet_id": "4667d704-8a8d-4f37-b4f8-c4ba382f2dfb",
        "name": "Phantasmata and Higher Cognitive Functions",
        "description": "How does Aristotle argue in De Anima Book III, De Memoria et Reminiscentia, and Metaphysics that phantasmata (mental images or appearances) are causally necessary for thinking (noesis), recollection (anamnesis), and imagination (phantasia in its productive sense)? What is the famous claim that the soul never thinks without phantasmata, and how do phantasmata function as the material or quasi-perceptual basis for abstract thought, memory, and deliberation? How does this complete the causal chain from motion through perception to intellection in Aristotle's philosophical psychology?",
        "facet_role": "core",
        "evidence_policy_for_kind": {},
        "archived": false,
        "atoms_mode": "analytics",
        "reverse_check_mode": "warn"
      }
    ],
